# Netlify response headers for the CarbonFlow production build.
#
# Security headers apply to every response:
#   nosniff              stops a browser from re-interpreting a response's
#                        declared content type, which closes MIME-sniffing
#                        vectors against user-supplied evidence downloads.
#   strict-origin-when-cross-origin
#                        keeps the full referrer on same-origin navigation and
#                        trims it cross-origin, so no tenant path leaks to a
#                        third-party host.
#
# Caching is per-path:
#   Vite writes content-hashed filenames into dist/assets/ (for example
#   DashboardView-1XQibnzZ.js). A hash in the name means the bytes at that URL
#   can never change, so it is safe to let the browser and the CDN keep them for
#   a year. This matters more after the workspace was split into lazy chunks: a
#   returning visitor reuses every cached chunk instead of re-downloading them.
#
#   index.html is the opposite case. It is the only unhashed document and it
#   points at the current hashed filenames, so it must always be revalidated —
#   caching it could pin a visitor to chunk URLs that a later deploy has
#   deleted, which shows up as a blank page.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate